-## Subtopics
-
-### NanoVolume Pass
-A specific shader module used within the shading pass to handle volume-specific ray marching or density evaluation, often integrated with the Custom Pass pipeline for hybrid rendering. It sets itself apart by providing a standardized interface for density sampling that decouples volume data access from lighting calculations. Choose it when integrating sparse voxel octrees into a Unity HDRP environment. See [NanoVolume Pass](981-plug-and-play-ray-marching-and-rendering.md).
-
-### Octree DDA Traversal
-The underlying geometric traversal algorithm used in the Pos/Norm pass to find hit positions in the volume octree, utilizing stack-based depth-first search. It sets itself apart by operating directly on DAG/Octree structures without explicit mesh generation. Choose it when rendering massive sparse volumetric datasets where explicit geometry is unavailable. See [Octree DDA Traversal](8936-ray-centric-and-hardware-accelerated-rendering.md).
+*   **Background Pixels:** If depth value is $\le 0$ or hit position is $(0,0,0)$, discard pixel or render sky color [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Normal Orientation:** Normals must be flipped if they point away from the camera ($\mathbf{n} \cdot \mathbf{camDir} > 0$) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Use `GL_RGBA32F` for depth and position buffers to avoid precision loss in large scenes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Material IDs:** Handle invalid material IDs (e.g., 255) with debug colors [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to screen resolution ($O(W \times H)$) per pass, with a constant number of passes (typically 6) [G]. The cost of the material lookup pass depends on octree depth but is generally fast due to texture buffer access [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to screen resolution [G]. Requires 7 FBOs (or 6 if materials are disabled) [S1, unofficial]. For a 1920x1080 screen with 32-bit floats, this is approximately $7 \times 1920 \times 1080 \times 4 \text{ bytes} \approx 58 \text{ MB}$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Highly dependent on GPU fill rate due to full-screen passes. SSAO pass is computationally expensive due to multiple texture fetches and matrix multiplications per sample [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Modular design allows enabling/disabling effects (SSAO, materials) independently. Efficient for large volumes where explicit geometry is not available [G].
-*   **Limitations:** Screen-space techniques (SSAO, normal estimation from depth) suffer from edge artifacts and holes. Requires high-precision depth buffers for accurate normal estimation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time visualization of large scientific datasets (NanoVDB) where visual quality (SSAO, shadows) is prioritized over absolute geometric precision [G].
-*   **When not to use:** Scenes with significant transparency where depth-based normal estimation fails, or when hardware does not support the required shader features (e.g., `#pragma use_dxc`) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $P$ is the number of passes (typically 5-7) [S1, S2, unofficial]. Each pass is $O(N)$ for screen-space operations, except volume traversal which depends on tree depth [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot B)$, where $B$ is the number of bytes per pixel per buffer. With 7 FBOs and 32-bit floats, this is approximately $7 \times 4 \times W \times H$ bytes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Real-time on modern GPUs for resolutions up to 4K, depending on scene complexity and SSAO sample count [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Modular, allows independent tuning of effects (SSAO, shadows, materials), leverages hardware rasterization for depth/position [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory bandwidth usage due to multiple FBOs, not suitable for extremely large scenes without tiling, SSAO quality depends on sample count [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Custom Pass Rendering pipeline originates from the **SSVDAG** (Sparse Stochastic Volume DAG) project and the **NanoVolume** renderer [S1, S2, unofficial]. It was developed to enable real-time rendering of massive volumetric datasets (terabytes in size) by leveraging sparse data structures (DAGs/Octrees) and modern GPU architectures [S1, S2, unofficial]. The pipeline was adapted for Unity's High Definition Render Pipeline (HDRP) to provide a flexible, multi-pass framework for volume visualization [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of stage separation and intermediate storage common to modern rendering pipelines [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Custom Pass Rendering originated from the SSVDAG (Sparse Sparse Voxel DAG) project by ajaspe [S1, unofficial] and was adapted for Unity by andersblomqvist [S2, unofficial]. It is used in real-time volumetric visualization tools, particularly for scientific data and game engines requiring high-quality volumetric effects [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
